@inject Profiler.Web.Data.SnapshotOptions Snapshots
@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Privacy";
}

<div class="page-wrap">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="page-header">
            <h1>🔒 How Profiler Protects Your Data</h1>
            <p>Privacy is not a setting here — it is the architecture.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="mb-2">What we store</h3>
                <p>
                    When you connect a source, we extract short interest labels (like
                    <code>language:python</code> or <code>genre:sci-fi</code>) and immediately
                    run them through a <strong>MinHash</strong> algorithm. Only the resulting
                    numeric signature is saved, together with the list of source names you
                    connected and your username and password hash (BCrypt). That's the whole database.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="mb-2">What we never store</h3>
                <ul style="padding-left:1.25rem">
                    <li>Raw interest data — book titles, watch history, starred repos, playlists</li>
                    <li>Access tokens and API keys — they are used once to fetch your data and discarded</li>
                    <li>Uploaded CSV files — parsed in memory, never written to disk</li>
                    <li>Email addresses or any contact details — an account is just a username</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="mb-2">Why matching still works</h3>
                <p>
                    Two MinHash signatures can be compared to estimate how similar the underlying
                    interest sets are (Jaccard similarity) without either side revealing what those
                    interests are. Other users only ever see your username, a similarity percentage,
                    and which source types you have in common.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted mb-0">
                    A signature is not reversible on its own, and it is additionally hashed with a
                    secret held by this server and never stored beside it — so a copy of the database
                    alone cannot be tested against a list of guessed interests to see which ones
                    were yours.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="mb-2">You stay in control</h3>
                <p>
                    Disconnect any source from your dashboard at any time — its signature is
                    deleted immediately and your fingerprint is rebuilt without it. You can also
                    delete your entire account from the dashboard, which permanently removes your
                    user record, fingerprint, and every per-source signature.
                </p>
                @if (Snapshots.Enabled)
                {
                    <p class="text-muted mb-0">
                        For disaster recovery this server keeps rolling snapshots of its database for up to
                        <strong>@Snapshots.RetentionDays days</strong>. They hold the same signatures as the live
                        database — never raw data — stay on the same server, and anything you delete leaves
                        them on that schedule.
                    </p>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
